WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Each kidney filter sits between two tiny vessels.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.360
Squeeze the one that drains it, and filtration can rise or fall.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.740
Which, and why?

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.020
The filter is a glomerulus, a tuft of capillaries cupped in Bowman's capsule.

5
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.780
Plasma is pushed out through a wall of three layers.

6
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.980
Water and small solutes pass freely.

7
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.480
Plasma protein mostly stays behind.

8
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:32.723
Filtrate leaves its protein behind.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:37.903
So the plasma flowing on carries the same protein in less water.

10
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:42.383
Along the capillary, the protein grows more concentrated.

11
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:46.207
Picture two lines along the capillary.

12
00:00:47.407 --> 00:00:52.087
The push is the capillary's pressure, less a small back-pressure in Bowman's space.

13
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:56.167
Between two arterioles, it stays nearly flat.

14
00:00:57.387 --> 00:00:59.807
The pull is the protein, holding water in.

15
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:02.427
It starts lower and rises.

16
00:01:03.627 --> 00:01:06.347
Fluid filters wherever the push beats the pull.

17
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.020
If enough water leaves, the pull rises to meet the push before the far end.

18
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.080
Beyond that point, nothing filters.

19
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.080
In mildly water-deprived rats, the pull typically caught up.

20
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.520
Whether human glomeruli do is unknown.

21
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.983
So filtration depends on two things.

22
00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:29.403
How high the pressure is.

23
00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:34.923
And how much plasma flows through, which slows the concentrating of the protein.

24
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:40.210
Blood enters through the afferent arteriole and leaves through the efferent

25
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:46.610
arteriole. Picture a hose: a tap near the spigot, a nozzle at the far end, and a

26
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:47.610
leaky stretch between.

27
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.200
Here is the one idea.

28
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.800
The two arterioles set both pressure and flow.

29
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.980
Their total resistance sets the flow.

30
00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.380
The efferent share of that resistance sets the pressure left in the filter.

31
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.360
So the afferent moves pressure and flow the same way.

32
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.480
The efferent moves them opposite ways.

33
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.150
Now squeeze the efferent arteriole harder, and harder again.

34
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.270
The pressure in the filter keeps climbing.

35
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.470
Does filtration keep climbing with it?

36
00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:30.527
Not forever. Each squeeze also cuts the flow.

37
00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:36.687
With less plasma passing, the protein concentrates sooner, so the pull catches up

38
00:02:36.687 --> 00:02:42.507
earlier. And the pressure can never exceed arterial pressure, so its gain levels

39
00:02:42.507 --> 00:02:43.507
off.

40
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.520
So a mild squeeze raises filtration slightly.

41
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.060
A hard squeeze lowers it from its peak.

42
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.140
Throughout, a larger share of the plasma is filtered: the filtration fraction rises.

43
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.770
That is the answer to the opening puzzle.

44
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.730
Filtering needs push to beat pull.

45
00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.210
The arterioles' combined resistance decides how much plasma arrives.

46
00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.630
Where that resistance sits decides how hard it is pushed.
